            "topic": "Digital Signal Processing",
            "title": "Z-Transform and Region of Convergence",
            "question": "A discrete-time Linear Time-Invariant (LTI) system has a transfer function $H(z) = \\frac{1}{1 - 0.5z^{-1}}$. Which of the following defines the Region of Convergence (ROC) for the system to be both causal and stable?",
            "options": [
                {
                    "label": "A",
                    "text": "$|z| < 0.5$",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "B",
                    "text": "$|z| > 0.5$",
                    "is_correct": true
                },
                {
                    "label": "C",
                    "text": "$|z| > 1$",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "D",
                    "text": "$0.5 < |z| < 1$",
                    "is_correct": false
                }
            ],
            "solution": {
                "steps": [
                    {
                        "title": "Locate the Pole",
                        "content": "The denominator is $1 - 0.5z^{-1}$. Setting it to zero gives $z = 0.5$. The system has a single pole at $z = 0.5$."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "Determine Condition for Causality",
                        "content": "For a causal system, the impulse response is right-sided, meaning the ROC must be the exterior of a circle enclosing the outermost pole. Hence, ROC: $|z| > 0.5$."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "Check Condition for Stability",
                        "content": "For a system to be stable, the ROC must include the unit circle ($|z| = 1$). Since $1 > 0.5$, the unit circle is included in $|z| > 0.5$."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "Conclusion",
                        "content": "The ROC $|z| > 0.5$ satisfies both causality and stability conditions."
                    }
                ],
                "solution_image": "",
                "video_explanation": "",
                "final_answer": "B"
            },
